WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.004
If now on Mac OS, you happened
to click on Advanced here,

00:00:03.004 --> 00:00:04.920
you'll see some additional
settings and you'll

00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:09.180
see that my IP address is
again 10.0.1.34 in this case,

00:00:09.180 --> 00:00:11.970
you'll see a subnet mask which
is used to decide whether or not

00:00:11.970 --> 00:00:14.280
some other computer is on
the same network as you,

00:00:14.280 --> 00:00:19.200
and then most importantly, you'll
see router, sometimes called gateway.

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:22.440
And in this case, it seems
that my gateway has an address,

00:00:22.440 --> 00:00:24.970
or my router has an address of 10.0.1.1.

00:00:24.970 --> 00:00:27.270
So that too of course is an IP address.

00:00:27.270 --> 00:00:30.930
And a router, as the name
suggests, is responsible for doing

00:00:30.930 --> 00:00:34.030
this kind of thing, routing
data in some direction.

00:00:34.030 --> 00:00:36.450
And if you run Windows, here's
what a similar screen might

00:00:36.450 --> 00:00:40.540
look like on that operating system which
shows, of course, your IPv4 address,

00:00:40.540 --> 00:00:44.440
and in this case, multiple
addresses for DNS servers.

00:00:44.440 --> 00:00:46.860
Router's purpose in
life is to be computers

00:00:46.860 --> 00:00:50.460
on the internet that have bunches
of wires usually coming into them

00:00:50.460 --> 00:00:52.800
and going out of them,
and they have essentially

00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:56.400
kind of a table, like a big
list, like an Excel spreadsheet,

00:00:56.400 --> 00:00:59.970
inside of themselves like inside
the RAM, Random Access Memory.

00:00:59.970 --> 00:01:03.840
And that table, generally has at
least like two columns, conceptually.

00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:06.780
One of which has an IP
address or a prefix,

00:01:06.780 --> 00:01:10.350
the first few numbers of an IP
address, and then some explanation

00:01:10.350 --> 00:01:13.860
of where data should be
routed to if it's destined

00:01:13.860 --> 00:01:15.680
for that IP address or that prefix.

00:01:15.680 --> 00:01:17.430
So maybe if an IP
address starts with one,

00:01:17.430 --> 00:01:19.410
it should go that way out that cable.

00:01:19.410 --> 00:01:22.290
Or if it starts with two, it
should go that way instead.

00:01:22.290 --> 00:01:25.560
Routers' purpose in life is to
route data in some direction

00:01:25.560 --> 00:01:29.950
to some next hop, or that is
to say to some next router.

00:01:29.950 --> 00:01:35.670
And so this means that this Mac
here with IP address 10.0.1.34

00:01:35.670 --> 00:01:37.890
is preconfigured by DHCP--

00:01:37.890 --> 00:01:42.000
which again, came from my ISP, or
from my university, or company--

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:44.910
is going to go to either local
computers on the internet

00:01:44.910 --> 00:01:47.520
if I happen to be talking
to another Mac or PC

00:01:47.520 --> 00:01:50.490
maybe to transfer file just
a few feet or somewhere else

00:01:50.490 --> 00:01:52.380
on campus or in the office.

00:01:52.380 --> 00:01:55.630
But if it's destined for somewhere
in the outside world like Google.com,

00:01:55.630 --> 00:01:58.650
well that's where the router comes
in, because routers purpose in life

00:01:58.650 --> 00:02:02.030
is to get data toward
another destination.

00:02:02.030 --> 00:02:07.280
And my little old laptop frankly doesn't
know where in the world Google.com is,

00:02:07.280 --> 00:02:10.280
but maybe this router does because
that's its purpose in life.

00:02:10.280 --> 00:02:14.070
And frankly, if that router
doesn't know, no big deal.

00:02:14.070 --> 00:02:15.590
There's other routers in the world.

00:02:15.590 --> 00:02:19.250
And so long as that router can
route data to some other server,

00:02:19.250 --> 00:02:23.570
well then hopefully that other router
can get data closer to its destination.

00:02:23.570 --> 00:02:27.640
And hopefully indeed, within some
number of hops, some number of steps,

00:02:27.640 --> 00:02:30.540
transmissions of packets from
one router to another to another,

00:02:30.540 --> 00:02:32.040
the data will reach its destination.
